# Static Effects — Spec
 
## Problem statement
The user can add static effects that do one of two things:
Buff: All creatures of subtype X get +X
Type Change: All creatures of subtype X are also subtype Y
There is also a removeDead function that destroys all creatures with toughness 0 or less. 
It is called after the initial boardstate is created.
If those creatures are tokens, they disappear, however if not they are moved to the graveyard.

## Types involved
- Game: (loadBoardstate, add map for buffs, set of pairs for type changes)
- Zone: (removePermanent, buffCreaturesType, copyPermanentAt, typalImply)

## Public interface
- void buffCreaturesType(int amt, std::string);
  // applies the buffs in the map of buffs that Game owns
- void typalImply(std::string type1, std::string type2);
  // applies type changes in set of type changes
 
## Inputs and outputs
- Input: Buff: creature subtype
         Typechange: creature subtype1 creature subtype2
- Output: No output, just update of battlefield and graveyard
 
## Edge cases
- Creatures of buffed type doesn't exist
- Type change tries to change creature type to the same creature type.
- Creature type a -> creature type b and creature type b -> creature type c;
- No dead creatures to remove.
 
## Three tests (English version)
- Normal: type change, then debuff changed type, then remove dead creature;
- Boundary: Creature type a -> creature type b and creature type b -> creature type c;
- Edge: No valid type changes or buffed added to input file.

## Design Decisions
Instead of using a set<pair<string,string>> for the type changes, will be using a vector,
since the order in which they are applied affects the types that creatures become.